# COMMITTEE#1 — CLAIM-0067 (PROJ-0037, data-systems/storage-encoding-semantics x feature-contract)
## EMPIRICAL CROSS-AREA PHENOMENON, effect=SUPPORT. Vote HONESTLY by role. Real 6/6 by role; committee#1 = gate to L1 or converge. NEW-COUPLING claim screened to clear the incremental-composition + verify-the-composition + framework-issue-tracker gates — judge the altitude squarely.

## ★★★ THE #1 STRESS-TESTS (orchestrator flags FRONT-AND-CENTER — the killers this claim must beat; a SIBLING claim CLAIM-0066 just died RED on the issue-tracker miss, so apply these hard):
A. FRAMEWORK-ISSUE-TRACKER PRIOR-ART (the CLAIM-0066 kill): is there a pandas/pyarrow GitHub issue or merged PR reporting "astype('category') gives unstable .cat.codes across files/shards with different observed subsets" framed as a CROSS-SHARD FEATURE-CONTRACT bug? The researcher reports the per-file sort behavior is DOCUMENTED (each file's codes are stable + sorted), but the CROSS-SHARD-instability-as-a-silent-ML-feature-contract framing was not found as an issue/PR. SCRUTINIZE: search the pandas issue tracker for ".cat.codes stable across files" / "categorical codes differ between parquet files" / "category dtype concat codes". If maintainers have a triaged issue or a fix, it's RED like CLAIM-0066.
B. INCREMENTAL-COMPOSITION (sharpened, false-by-documentation): does EITHER endpoint's OFFICIAL DOCS warn about THIS outcome? pandas docs DO document that astype('category') sorts per-file + that .cat.codes are per-categorical-dtype — BUT do they warn that two SEPARATELY-CAST shards produce DIFFERENT codes for the same string, silently corrupting a cross-shard ML feature? If the docs warn "don't use .cat.codes as a portable encoding across datasets," the neither-predicts leg weakens. The claim's defense: A only emits STRINGS (round-trip fine, never an integer contract); B's .cat.codes is stable WITHIN a file so the per-file docs don't fire the cross-shard alarm; B never FITS an encoder so the fit-on-train/transform-on-serve discipline never engages. Is that genuinely non-obvious (CLAIM-0059 altitude) or documented folklore (the '.cat.codes isn't portable' gotcha = CLAIM-0064/0066 pattern)?
C. NOT-STANDARD-LEAKAGE: confirm this is distinct from train-test leakage / target-encoding leakage (Kapoor-Narayanan 2023) — those involve a FITTED estimator/contract; here NO estimator is fitted, the contract is set by the STORAGE cast. Is that distinction real + sufficient?

## CLAIM
A storage team's per-shard df[col].astype("category") (compression) silently sets an UNSTABLE .cat.codes integer feature contract across shards: the cast SORTS over the OBSERVED values per shard, so categories co-occurring across shards with DIFFERENT observed subsets get DIFFERENT codes (100% of common categories), collapsing a tree model's R^2 on overlapping categories with NO error raised. NULL EXIT: full alphabet per shard -> codes stable -> deltaR2=0 (conditional on PARTIAL OBSERVATION = the common time/geo-partitioned/streaming case; single-file pipelines immune).

## L0 RESULT (EXP-0080; CPU; pinned CURRENT pandas 3.0.3 / pyarrow 24.0.0 / sklearn 1.9.0 / numpy 2.4.6 — NEWER than scout's 2.3.3/21.0.0, composition STILL HOLDS)
COMPOSITION VERIFIED (a-c, probes saved): (a) astype("category") sorts over OBSERVED values only — shard1 obs c00..c39, shard2 c10..c49 -> code('c20')=20 vs 10, 30/30=100% common codes differ; (a-null) FULL alphabet both shards (even reversed input) -> 0/50 differ (bug CONDITIONAL on partial observation). (b) codes SURVIVE parquet round-trip (30/30 still differ post-round-trip). (c) pyarrow.dictionary_encode first-seen order round-trips to .cat.codes -> independent 2nd divergence source.
MAIN (50 cats, latent theta_k~N(0,1), y=theta_cat+N(0,0.5), realistic global-map R^2~0.78 NOT inflated; 2 partial-overlap shards each parquet-roundtripped; HistGradientBoosting + RF cross-check; 12 seeds):
★ PARTIAL-OBSERVATION SWEEP (astype, HGB) — monotonicity smoking gun:
 f=0.0: R2(ctrl)=0.780, R2(seam)=0.780, deltaR2=0.000 (0% codes differ) — null boundary EXACT.
 f=0.1: deltaR2=0.966 (61%); f=0.2: 1.067 (73%); f=0.4: 1.415 (74%); f=0.6: 1.046 (high-variance tail, disclosed).
 deltaR2 rises STRICTLY MONOTONIC 0->0.4; seam R2 goes NEGATIVE (-0.2 to -0.68, worse than predicting the mean) with ZERO error. RF cross-check f=0.4: deltaR2=1.414 (vs HGB 1.415) -> not model-specific.
2ND ARM (pyarrow dictencode_shuffled, genuine arrival-order): deltaR2 high even at f=0 (1.535, 99% codes differ) -> first-seen path is UNCONDITIONALLY unstable (arrival-order gated), mechanistically DISTINCT from astype (partial-observation gated). Both collapse R2 with no error.
PRE-REGISTERED IT-MATTERS all met (deltaR2>0.2 at f>=0.2; monotone 0->0.4; f=0 control deltaR2=0.000; composition a/b/c pass). COULD-FAIL audits all clean (astype not stable across partial-obs; codes survive parquet; deltaR2>=1.0 for all f>=0.1; pandas 3.0 not fixed; realistic SNR not engineered).

## PRIOR-ART: sklearn LabelEncoder/OrdinalEncoder teach fit-on-train/transform-on-serve but target ENCODER OBJECTS; .cat.codes is a DTYPE PROPERTY set by the storage layer so the guidance never engages. Active leakage lit (Kapoor-Narayanan 2023, fit-before-split, target-encoding) = within-dataset train/test CONTAMINATION with a fitted contract -> orthogonal (NO fitted estimator here). No pandas doc found warning astype-category codes are observation-subset-dependent ACROSS files as an ML-feature hazard. Committee MUST scrutinize the pandas/pyarrow ISSUE TRACKER (stress-test A) for a missed report/fix.

## ORCHESTRATOR NOTE (NOT advocacy): exceptionally clean, well-controlled L0 — composition verified in CURRENT pandas 3.0.3 (verify-the-composition honored), monotonicity smoking gun with an EXACT f=0 null boundary, realistic SNR (anti-rigging), RF cross-check, 2 distinct divergence arms, all could-fail audits clean. The make-or-break is the 3 stress-tests: (A) is there a pandas issue/PR reporting this cross-shard .cat.codes-as-feature-contract instability (-> RED like CLAIM-0066 if so)? (B) is it a non-obvious coupling (CLAIM-0059 altitude) or the documented '.cat.codes isn't portable' folklore? (C) is the no-fitted-estimator distinction from standard leakage real+sufficient? If it clears all three -> advance to L1 (real partitioned public dataset e.g. Criteo/NYC-taxi end-to-end + a real ML pipeline that consumes .cat.codes cross-shard). If a pandas issue owns it or it's documented folklore -> RED/YELLOW. Judge honestly. Real 6/6 by role. Prompt version v003.
